[Music]  hello ladies and gentlemen and welcome  to today's broadcast on challenges of  establishing laboratory developed tests  by dr vijay singh  i'm bill and leicher from thermo fisher  scientific and i'll be moderating  today's event  before we begin i would like to  encourage you to engage with us you can  submit as many questions as you want at  any time you want during the  presentation  simply type your question in the q a box  on the left hand side of your  presentation screen and click submit  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for at the end of the  presentation  please welcome vijay  hello everyone uh  today i'll be talking about  our experience here at healthtrack rx um  we are a speciality infectious disease  testing lab right outside dallas  and uh so i'll be talking about our  experience in establishing laboratory  developed tests or ldt's  specifically for disease diagnostics  the presentation today  will be covering in very broad terms uh  the three basic questions that basically  define anything in life the what why and  how  of  establishing an ldt in your lab  so what is an ldt  as the fda defines it now technically an  ldt is not fda approved and is defined  as an in-vitro diagnostic test that is  developed validated and commercialized  for use within a single laboratory  and for anyone who is interested in  developing ldps for use in their lab i  would highly recommend  the website that you see  on the on the slide right now  to visit this website because um it has  some really great information  of what defines an ldp and and how to  get started  so now the question is  why are ldt is needed now very often  and i'll show you some examples later it  is the case that certain diagnostic  tests are not available commercially and  there can be a number of reasons for  that uh maybe it's a rare target that  you're interested in  or the clinical utility  of that particular test is uh not very  well defined  and you know and it is simply not  commercially viable to bring those tests  to market keep in mind that  developing and getting full fda approval  for a single test  you know for the manufacturer it can  cost  upwards of  10 million dollars  uh you know  to to get full fda approved test  and sometimes  some you'll find that the fda approved  test that is available on the market you  know  its performance is inferior as compared  to  some of the other ldt's that are running  and  and the amount of flexibility  that one has in establishing an ldp with  respect to using multiple manufacturers  and sources that will bring together  that test  that  all together may sometimes  makes an ldp both attractive and  necessary  so now  that you have decided to bring the pain  of establishing an ldt into your life  how does one go about doing that and the  very first thing that you do is that you  look for  you know the regulations and the  guidelines that define this process and  this is the very point that it begins to  you know get confusing because you have  all these very different regulating and  guiding authorities that define both the  test characteristics and the laboratory  practices  now within the us uh the ultimate  authority on ldt's is the fda or the  food and drug administration  however what the fda does  is that it  practices  enforcement discretion  and uh  because there are there is clear and  i'll be talking about that in greater  detail in the next few slides and  individual state laws that regulate the  functioning of the labs and the  fda  lets these regulating bodies control the  tests that are being run in the lab  however you know  once you are getting into the process of  developing an ldt what you'll realize is  that  all of these regulating authorities  or guiding authorities  have  certain set guidelines and there is  you'll find there is a major overlap  in  these guidelines and any of the  resources that are provided by these  authorities can be a good starting point  so  here in the us uh it's the clinical  laboratory improvement amendments or  clear  which was established in 1988  that regulates all testing of human  samples in a laboratory  and  these guidelines went underwent  extensive revisions in 2003 which is now  called the  final of the clear final rule  and  it defines the various test performance  characteristics that need to be  established for bringing an ldt on board  but what you should also keep in mind  that depending on where you are located  you have you may have more extensive and  stringent uh local state laws so one  should also keep that in mind while  trying to develop these tests  now  here is very broadly speaking uh the  different test performance  characteristics  that are defined by the 2003 final rule  right we're talking about the reportable  range the analytical  sensitivity and specificity of the test  the  precision of that test on a day-to-day  or over a time period because remember  you can be precisely wrong about  something the accuracy of the test and  the reference intervals now  i'm not going to get into the technical  details as uh these guidelines are  widely and very clearly available  and and that would entail a whole  different presentation  but  the important point that one has to keep  in mind is that according to clear  one has to establish now or be not in  the same great detail but you have to  establish these test performance  characteristics  even for fba approved tests in your  laboratory okay  so what was our experience here at  healthtrack rx in developing some of  these ldt's  it was a somewhere towards the end of  2017 when we launched our large  multiplex syndromic real-time pcr  test panels  that cover a wide variety  of  tests with respect to respiratory  urinary tract sexually transmitted  infections  gastrointestinal and  wound dermatology and ophthalmology  based infections  and  this was our guiding principle  that what we wanted to do was  to develop a testing strategy that  incorporated a multiplexed real-time  pcr-based syndromic panel approach  that saved both time and money  again for both the patients  and their providers  that ultimately results  in better clinical decisions with  respect to patient care  so in brief  the idea was to provide the maximum  amount of clinically relevant  information to both the patient and the  provider  and to do so  at a reasonable cost again to the  patient  and  to us  at a reasonable cost and profit margin  so to anyone who's thinking about  establishing ldt's in your lab  right the first thing that you should do  is that you should you need to determine  a testing strategy  that works best for you  not only as a scientific exercise and  i'll be you know  going into some details on that you know  again not just as a scientific exercise  but as a business decision also  and uh it is not just the test or the  assay alone  that has to be the focus it is the whole  workflow  within the lab  that feeds into that test that needs to  be streamlined  in such a manner that the wastage of  time money and resources is minimized  and the information that is generated  for both the provider and the patient is  maximized  and  again i'll be bringing up this point  again and again is that  as you begin to think about developing  these strategies these workflows or  bringing these tests on board right  you would want to work very closely with  your asset manufacturer or your test  developers  right  so  what was our approach  when we looked at the whole  workflow right here is an example  of our  respiratory  infection panel or the rpp panel  and  when you look at these targets  i can tell you  with a very high degree of confidence  that if you go about looking for  fda approved tests on these  there are maybe one or two  non-viral targets that are fda approved  and depending on the manufacturer that  you may want to work with  you may have  anywhere from two to six or maybe seven  viral targets  that are  that have fda approved tests available  and uh  when you looked at the very extensive  list of antibiotic resistance markers  that we have  only two  of those targets are really reimbursed  by the players  right  if you would  go about looking for  again fda approved or clear wave test  such an extensive list is simply not  available  however  what we have seen in a number of cases  you know there is a  huge amount of clinically relevant  information  with respect to you know the pathogens  or the infections that might be present  in a patient sample that would simply be  missed  by  a lot of these uh  point of care test or fda approved tests  and uh  we published a paper in 2019 detailing  you know the severe limitation of uh  some of these point-of-care tests up to  50 of the time where clinically relevant  infections would be missed  by some of these tests and so that  in itself um  validated  our approach  towards developing the ldt's  and uh  to talk about the workflow as i said  everything else that flows  into that assay is also equally  important so uh  we spent a significant amount of time  and resources in  developing a lysis and transport medium  and  i can say that we've sort of achieved  true synergy  wherein we use this single medium for  all our different sample types so  whether it's rpp sti wound gi whatever  the sample type may be we use this  single  uh  medium for transport and that allows for  uh you know in transit lysis also  and again like i said as a business  decision when we looked at developing  the ldt's what we realized that you know  that running these extensive panels  would make sense at a certain  patient volume or sample volume  and for that  it became very very apparent very soon  is that we would need automation at  different levels of the workflow and one  of that point which was proving to be a  big bottleneck was the extraction of the  nucleic acid from the sample so we spent  again a significant amount of time  in uh  developing automated processes for the  extraction  and which is once we have the nucleic  acid in hand we follow it up with  reverse transcription pre-amplification  of the samples because we found or what  we  ultimately went with was a nanofluidic  real-time pcr based platform  which allowed for really high throughput  uh  performance of the pci  and then followed by the that real-time  pc that i was talking about ultimately  leading to the report which is the  product  that we put out for the providers and  the patient  again we have streamlined this workflow  which again minimizes  any wastage with respect to time and  resources for all our different sample  types  okay  looking at you know some of the  examples with respect to the performance  characteristics  and herein i would emphasize the point  of working closely with your  asset manufacturers  is that one of the biggest  you know stumbling blocks that labs face  in  in developing any extensive panels  is finding the reference material of  you know  when you use that reference material to  establish some of those test  characteristics and it can be very very  difficult to find true relevant  reference material so the manufacturer  that we work with was able to provide us  in a timely fashion you know  all of our target sequences  at a defined known value  what that allowed us to do was to  establish the limit of detection of our  assays and to basically  uh do good dilution assays that allowed  us to  look at you know  a good linear correlation for all of our  assay targets so you can see  what we received was 10 raised to power  5  copies of our target sequences which we  could very quickly do a good  dilution assay and see very very good  sensitivity for the test  and in the meantime we kept working with  the  a lot of sources so public health  departments hospitals clinics  in  sourcing  true known patient samples  with the clearly identified infections  and uh some of the relevant examples are  here and you can see that we could  establish very good linear correlation  for all of our assays and that allowed  us  to  you know  define a lot of our  test characteristics that are laid down  by the different guidelines whether they  be from clear cap  or iso or the fda  and  now  you know i'll be talking about uh a more  you know  timely example which was the  establishing of a code 19 test here at  uh healthtrack rx  and uh  it was uh somewhere  in february of 2020  when uh the covert 19  was declared as a public health  emergency and as a pandemic  and so we moved very fast now keep in  mind that our experience was more with  these multiplexed large panels and we  never uh  till this point we haven't really done  any  single target single pathogen type pcr  tests in the lab  and but we moved fairly quickly  in trying to establish this test  and  when we started generating data for this  test in the lab  the emergency use authorization that was  given by the fda  was very limited it was only for a  particular extraction type  it was only on a particular machine the  abi 7500 which we did not have in the  lab  and uh so what the fda suggested was  that if you wanted to do anything else  they defined it as a bridging study  because they had laid down the the test  guidelines of the characteristics or the  performance characteristics of  these tests  and  so we started with the  defining  all the various different sample types  you know again the  automated rna extraction  and the real-time  pcr performance on  machines that were frankly different  and we didn't have any resources at that  moment of getting any of those machines  in the lab and frankly we'd not have the  time to validate those new machines okay  and then the assay  was also different  from what was approved at that point  uh and again you know the different data  points and different test  characteristics were established so here  is our whole table for the limit of  detection and you can see that it was uh  established at uh  10 raised to power one copies of the  viral target per microliter  and it's not that we could not  detect it at any lower values if you  would go that we went right down to 10  raised to power 0.1 copies per  microliter  of our  target however  we made a decision of uh  tapping our the limit of our test  at 35 ct values because if you look at  the linear equations even though this  is a great  uh linear  correlation for the different assays or  the different targets  uh you can see  that you know if you look at the  equations themselves the moment you  start getting beyond  the 35 ct value  uh  you the  the clear determination  of whether the sample is positive or  negative  would become questionable  and then  we had  all of our samples from the 2019 study  saved in a deep freezer  and  that allowed us to do  a good specificity study when you know  all the other syndromic targets  we found that there was uh  no interference  from those  and uh  overall the specificity of uh  testing a difficult uh  sample type like the sputum samples as  opposed to  a nasal or an oropharyngeal swab  uh put together the predictive value of  our test both positive and negative was  found to be hundred percent and then at  the same time we move very quickly to  do interlab correlations  and uh you know  for uh  as per fda guidelines we took the  difficult sample type which was the  sputum and  we correlated our results with  another lab  and that allowed us  you know for the granting of our  eua and uh since that time i would say  in the last  18 months or so  we have performed  nearly 2 million covert tests  and so why i gave this particular  example was that when we were generating  this data with respect to filing for the  eua application we were also  following  all the guidelines  from  with respect to a capricorn you know  inspection and uh the idea was that all  the technical repetitions all the  biological repetitions all the precision  studies the accuracy studies that are  needed that we had all of that data on  files for our cap inspection and when  our  cap inspection window opened in february  of this year  we passed it with flying colors because  we already had that data  and  you know for people who may find it  difficult and again  yes we were coming from a place of high  experience but at the same time this was  a new approach for us but all of this  work you know  if one thinks that it is difficult yes  it is however if you are planning and uh  as i used to say in a previous life to  all of my students that one month on the  bench will save you two hours in the  library  you know  it's an excruciating amount of planning  that goes into it  but all of this work with respect to  establishing the covert assay  was done in a period of 10 days  so with that  i'll uh summarize this presentation  that yes establishing an ldt can be a  very daunting task and if you look at  the different regulating bodies  and keeping in mind that you may have  you know  a particular state laws also governing  you  this can be very very confusing however  if you look at those  guidelines in a very broad sense these  are the basic principles of good  laboratory practice that are guiding you  so follow those guidelines and follow  those guidelines to a t keeping in mind  that you will have to pass an inspection  right and if you're doing that  again there can be limitations with  respect to resources  and uh what your testing strategy has to  be what your business strategy is right  you will have to make those decisions  however  the decision of establishing an ldt  in the lab should always keep the  patient interest  at its core right what is the clinical  utility of the test  how will that test  impact the patient life how will that  test provide all the clinically relevant  information to the provider to  to make the best decisions with respect  to the patient's care right that has to  be kept in mind right but after that  right  what you should always do you know you  have to remember  that this ultimately is is a business  decision okay  so you will have to bring all of that  together and the workflow in the lab  and uh the costs of running that test  and what would be the reimbursements all  of those different things have to come  together for you to make that decision  and again i cannot emphasize this enough  if you ask nicely and work closely with  the manufacturers of those assays  it can be a very easy task because like  i said before you may be resource  limited but realizes that people and  collaborators around you  will have  expertise that you may not possess at  that time and they will help you  bring together these ldt's that you know  to the market  to your patients to your providers  and it can be you know  a task that that is easier than it looks  uh and uh with that i would like to  thank uh  the whole infectious disease team here  at healthtrack rx  uh  again i'm privileged to work with the  such a great group of people  uh in the last one and a half years with  the pandemic  uh i have i've  i've never seen again a more committed  group of people they've been through  hell and back and i i see them very  tired all the time but uh you know  never wavering in their commitment to  the patients into the turnaround time  and i would like to thank all of you for  listening and i'll take any questions  that you may have thank you  thank you vijay for sharing your  personal experiences and  reflections of what it takes for a  clinical lab to bring a in-house  molecular test to market  we have received some great questions  already  vijay what advice would you give to a  laboratory looking to implement its  first ldt  again  great points to emphasize again  you know  the the first and the foremost  that they should look for again like i  said there are some great fda approved  tests available right  and uh  they have to see they have to keep in  mind that  what is the niche that they are  targeting you know  is it uh  clinics is it uh long-term care  facilities hospitals  or uh  you know  opd population and once you have that  clarity  and you know what sort of sample volume  you would be looking at  and then  what the market needs are where the gaps  are and they would want they want to  fulfill those gaps  then they need to define  the the testing strategy that would go  and again  it has to be with the realization that  you know some of  the time and the resource  that needs to be put into developing  these leds can be fairly extensive it  can be it can be expensive  for a lot of labs  so  first clearly define the need that you  want to fulfill and then  look at the resources that are available  to you  and again collaborate like i said one of  the  one of the big stumbling blocks in  defining the test characteristics is  finding the resource or the reference  material you know so work with the  public health departments in your area  work with hospitals you know connect  with the universities around you  and the clinics around you  and  like i said work very closely with your  asset developers if you're not doing it  in-house  and uh you know  all of those resources coming together  all of those expertise besides your own  coming together  will  will make this whole exercise easier  great we have a two-part question it  reads do you have any tips for someone  who is new or unfamiliar with panel  development and testing and are there  any resources you find particularly  helpful  so like i said if that has to be  you know  extensive planning and i can talk from  uh  from our own experience here is that  you need one  you know you can do a survey of the  clinicians around you  and uh  they will tell you what sort of tests  they are interested in  uh  do a extensive literature search  the idea with us was to  bring together you know a very high  percentile  of the pathogens representing each  syndrome  and then obviously at the same time we  did a market survey of the clinicians  around us  and uh you know  they pointed out certain targets that  were not a part of our original list but  we found that there was enough interest  in  the clinicians  for  for those particular tests or for those  particular pathogens for each panel type  and  again  i say this with the full realization  that you know  not all laboratories are going to have  those resources at their disposal or  they may not have the time or the money  to  to invest into that however  i would still very seriously suggest for  everyone to try and take that approach  because  in the long term  that would allow you to put a good  product out in the market  for the like i said i keep saying again  again for both the patient and the  provider  and  then how you design your workflow will  allow you to to do it at a at a price  point  that would again  maximize  the information  and minimize the cost  vijay how quickly are you able to change  your panel content and bring a new test  to offer to your clients  so the nature of  our large multiplex panels  the way they are engineered and the way  the technology is  uh it takes  anywhere from four to eight weeks if we  have to make extensive changes on our  panels  however you know depending on uh  i'll give you an example what we  realized with our sdi  panels was  that a large number  of the providers were  interested in gonorrhea chlamydia and  trichomonas test only  and  so  what we did was that we created a small  panel specifically for that  and we basically took it out of our  took these three targets out of our uh  large multiplex panel  and uh  again you know i think we'd already cut  our teeth with the covert assay and uh  if  you know from the standpoint of  manufacturing  uh  it took less than three weeks to do that  what uh what would you say is the single  most important thing to keep in mind  when developing a successful test  personally i would say that you need to  look at the  the clinical utility of that test  and uh  you know always keeping the  the patient's interest at heart  but  not losing you know  the  the perspective that yes it is a  business decision also  so one should uh  one has to bring in different aspects  there  is no one  true  fact or aspect that defines  the ldt  uh and  so you you will have to keep in mind  that is there  you know  a gap  in what is available to the patients  is there a certain market need  and again if you do decide to bring that  on board  you know what would be the implications  of running that test  uh with respect to  fair reimbursement  and even if those reimbursements are low  can you design a workflow that will  minimize your cost and would allow you  depending on the sample volume would  allow you to perform that test and can  you bring that or can you commercialize  that test within a reasonable profit  margin so all of those aspects  truly come together and so i wouldn't  say that there is one true aspect that  that is overarching and defines it  we we do have time for one more question  uh vijay what are the most  time-consuming or challenging parts of  the process for bringing a test to  market do you have any advice  so uh like i said uh  one of the uh you know it's the actual  planning of the assay itself  and uh  you know  once you have identified the system that  you want to work with  and uh  then  you know there are certain areas that  are  are more clearly defined like i would  say in the whole process the  nucleic acid extraction process  that's that's more well defined there  are certain known ways that in which you  do it and  you may have experience with one system  versus the other  and those are  easier still  however  the design of the panel itself  you know  and then uh depending on who your  manufacturer is  that can take a long amount of time  and  uh then  once you  once you have the asses in-house once  you have them in your hand  the  the thing that can take a long amount  a bigger amount of time  is finding the proper reference material  so i would suggest that one should start  looking for the certified reference  material whether it's through  you know  a known source like atcc which can give  you  the  the pathogens themselves  and  you can spike  the proper matrix  and do those tests  or if you work with the  synthetic pieces of dna  or you work with you know hospitals or  public health departments that can  provide you with those samples  or work closely with the universities in  your area  that will let me have some research  material available to them but that is  one aspect that takes  a  a long time  to bring together  and then again it's it's the planning of  those experiments so read those  guidelines carefully and they very very  clearly define  the  the basic minimum criteria of doing each  experiment  and what are the  repetitions and what sort of statistics  needs to be done on that data they  clearly define those so make sure that  your planning of those experiments is  done  you know  beforehand again follow those good  laboratory practices  and  it'll be a much much much easier task  thank you vijay that is all the time we  have for today as a reminder to our  audience  questions we did not have time for today  and those that are submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed via  the email address you provided at the  time of registration  so thank you again to our speaker vijay  singh and to everyone who participated  in this presentation we hope you enjoy  the rest of the event and please be sure  to check back frequently for new content  and presentations added throughout the  year  you

